7592186496222 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${X}_{1}∼N(11,2)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(-5,5)$ are independent, then what is the distribution of $Y=-2{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496222 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00d2e780-6ff9-5f4c-863c-7e7274f9ff9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496222 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=-2{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2} & ∼N((-2)\\cdot 11+4\\cdot (-5),{2}^{2}\\cdot 2+(-4)^{2}\\cdot 5) \\\\  & ∼N(-42,88).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y∼N(-42,88).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496225 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496225 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109663" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496225 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496225 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${X}_{1}∼N(-7,4)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(-3,10),$ then what is the distribution of $Y=3{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496225 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0705c6e0-425c-53a2-92bd-305a48e1d4f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496225 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$  and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nHowever, in this case, we don't know whether ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent. Therefore, we do not have enough information to determine the answer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496228 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496228 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109759" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496228 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496228 #edb/ref 1071 "In a technical support call center, waiting times are normally distributed with a mean of $15$ minutes and a standard deviation of $3$ minutes. The conversation times are normally distributed with a mean of $10$ minutes and a standard deviation of $4$ minutes. The time to solve a problem is the sum of the waiting time and the conversation time. What is the probability that it took more than $30$ minutes to solve the problem of a randomly selected caller? You may assume that waiting and conversation times are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ | $\\,0.8729\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8849\\,$ | $\\,0.8869\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496228 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c9343e5-40f1-5766-8346-83637c7ee445" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496228 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ denote the waiting time, and let $Y$ denote the conversation time. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(X+Y>30).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(15,{3}^{2})$ and $Y∼N(10,{4}^{2})$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+Y & ∼N(15+10,{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(25,25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X+Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =25$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nIn order to find $P(X+Y>30),$ we convert $X+Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>30) & =P(Z>\\frac{30-25}{5}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1)≈0.8413.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>30) & =1-Φ(1) \\\\  & ≈1-0.8413 \\\\  & ≈0.1587.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496254 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496254 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175411" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496254 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496254 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${X}_{1}∼N(1,3)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(1,2),$ then what is the distribution of $Y=-{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496254 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ad84c9b-528f-52d5-bcd2-180abda6b74b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496254 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nHowever, in this case, we don't know whether ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent. Therefore, we do not have enough information to determine the answer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496259 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496259 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496259 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496259 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${X}_{1}∼N(-10,{12}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(-5,{5}^{2}),$ then what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496259 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a0d2344-860b-5e90-a188-b68252dd0262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496259 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$  then \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nHowever, in this case, we don't know whether ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent. Therefore, we do not have enough information to determine the answer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496260 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496260 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109656" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496260 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496260 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${X}_{1}∼N(7,16)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(-2,4)$ are independent, then what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496260 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5cd38ce0-916c-531b-b472-b1dea997c072" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496260 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ then \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2} & ∼N(7-(-2),16+4) \\\\  & ∼N(9,20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $Y∼N(9,20).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496267 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496267 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109669" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496267 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496267 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${X}_{1}∼N(7,1)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(6,3)$ are independent and $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2},$ calculate $P(Y\\le 2.1).$ Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ | $\\,0.6480\\,$ | $\\,0.6517\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ | $\\,0.6808\\,$ | $\\,0.6844\\,$ | $\\,0.6879\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ | $\\,0.7224\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496267 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "65c490f4-ee79-5c66-84d0-39f33e6e9ec0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496267 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2})$, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2} & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(7-6,1+3) \\\\  & ∼N(1,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =1$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{4}=2.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(Y\\le 2.1).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 2.1) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{2.1-1}{2}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.55) \\\\  & =Φ(0.55)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.55)≈0.7088.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le 2.1)≈0.71.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496268 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496268 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496268 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496268 #edb/ref 1071 "A Japanese-style table tennis racket is made from a wooden blade covered with rubber on one side.  The weight of a blade is normally distributed with a mean of $80\\,\\text{g}$ and a variance of $31\\,{\\text{