{2}^{2})$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X-3Y & ∼N(10-3\\cdot 5,16+{3}^{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(-5,52).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X-3Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-5$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{52}.$\n\nIn order to find $P(X-3Y>0),$ we convert $X-3Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X-3Y>0) & =P(Z>\\frac{0+5}{\\sqrt{52}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.69) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.69)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.69)≈0.7549.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X-3Y>0) & ≈1-Φ(0.69) \\\\  & ≈1-0.7549 \\\\  & ≈0.2451.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the probability that the weight of a watermelon is more than $3$ times greater than the weight of a melon is $0.2451.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496316 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496316 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109726" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496316 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496316 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${X}_{1}∼N(-14,{20}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(-6,{15}^{2})$ are independent and $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2},$ calculate $P(-28<Y\\le 12).$ Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3821\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3783\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3745