{X}_{1}+{X}_{2} & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(-14-6,400+225) \\\\  & ∼N(-20,625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-20$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{625}=25.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(-28<Y\\le 12).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-28<Y\\le 12) & =P(\\frac{-28+20}{25}<Z\\le \\frac{12+20}{25}) \\\\  & =P(-0.32\\le Z\\le 1.28) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.28)-P(Z<-0.32) \\\\  & =Φ(1.28)-Φ(-0.32)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.32) & ≈0.3745 \\\\ Φ(1.28) & ≈0.8997.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-28<Y\\le 12) & =Φ(1.28)-Φ(-0.32) \\\\  & ≈0.8997-0.3745 \\\\  & ≈0.53.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496325 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496325 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109757" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496325 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496325 #edb/ref 1071 "A particular shop sells chickens and turkeys. The weight of a chicken is normally distributed with a mean of $1.5\\,\\text{kg}$ and a standard deviation of $0.1\\,\\text{kg}.$ The weight of a turkey is normally distributed with a mean of $8\\,\\text{kg}$ and a standard deviation of $0.4\\,\\text{kg}.$ If a chicken and a turkey are randomly selected, what is the probability that $5$ times the weight of the chicken is greater than the turkey's weight? You may assume that the weights are independent. \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ | $\\,0.7852\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ | $\\,0.8051\\,$ | $\\,0.8078\\,$ | $\\,0.8106\\,$ | $\\,0.8133\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ | $\\,0.8315\\,$ | $\\,0.8340\\,$ | $\\,0.8365\\,$ | $\\,0.8389\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496325 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7f40d31-3891-595e-9dc0-80e5713161b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496325 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ denote the weight of a chicken, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a turkey. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(5X>Y)=P(5X-Y>0).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(1.5,{0.1}^{2})$ and $Y∼N(8,{0.4}^{2}),$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5X-Y & ∼N(5\\cdot 1.5-8,{5}^{2}\\cdot {0.1}^{2}+{0.4}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(-0.5,0.41).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $5X-Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-0.5$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.41}.$\n\nTo find $P(5X-Y>0),$ we convert $5X-Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5X-Y>0) & =P(Z>\\frac{0-(-0.5)}{\\sqrt{0.41}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.78) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.78) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.78)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.78)≈0.7823.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5X-Y>0) & ≈1-Φ(0.78) \\\\  & ≈1-0.7823 \\\\  & 0.2177.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496327 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496327 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109648" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496327 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496327 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${X}_{1}∼N(-1,{6}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(5,{8}^{2})$ are independent, then what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496327 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb127e88-57e6-5641-837e-22d6b55ce8da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496327 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ then \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2} & ∼N(-1+5,{6}^{2}+{8}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(4,100) \\\\  & ∼N(4,{10}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $Y∼N(4,{10}^{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496332 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496332 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109671" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496332 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496332 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${X}_{1}∼N(8,{40}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(30,{30}^{2})$ are independent and $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2},$ calculate $P(-31\\le Y\\le 31).$ Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3557\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3520\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3483\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3936\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3897\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3859\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4325\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4286\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4247\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8289\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8315\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8340\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8531\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8554\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8577\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8749\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8770\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8790\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496332 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f962dd14-3b46-5fda-bac4-2a4b37326612" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496332 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2})$, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2} & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(8-30,{40}^{2}+{30}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(-22,2500).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-22$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{2500}=50.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(-31\\le Y\\le 31).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-31\\le Y\\le 31) & =P(\\frac{-31-(-22)}{50}\\le Z\\le \\frac{31-(-22)}{50}) \\\\  & =P(-0.18\\le Z\\le 1.06) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.06)-P(Z<-0.18) \\\\  & =Φ(1.06)-Φ(-0.18)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.18) & ≈0.4286 \\\\ Φ(1.06) & ≈0.8554\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-31\\le Y\\le 31) & =Φ(1.06)-Φ(-0.18) \\\\  & ≈0.8554-0.4286 \\\\  & ≈0.43.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
